{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019V79NL4x2cgJWTcmXWzqCi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Scope and limitations.}\\looseness=-1\n5\tAll findings are correlational (confounded by data distribution and optimization; $N = 2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions; Appendix~\\ref{sec:claim_evidence_appendix}); we analyze causal-decoder VLAs on LIBERO only (flow-matching~\\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\\cite{ghosh2024_octo} architectures may differ); all analyses are open-loop.\n6\tTrained $\\Delta R^2$ correlates with published success rates ($\\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) but is underpowered ($n = 9$; effect size is large, $|\\rho| > 0.5$); tracing collapse onset remains for future work.\n7\t\n8\t\\paragraph{Practical implications and conclusion.}\\looseness=-1\n9\t$\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ provides a cheap probe-based screening signal that can supplement rollout evaluation, though the observed correlation with published success rates remains non-significant at conventional levels ($\\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$, $n = 9$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).\n10\tThe flat VLA-to-VLA $\\Delta R^2$ profile justifies staged pipelines that preserve exit structure, and mid-layer features (peak at L9--L11) outperform exit-layer representations where collapse is pronounced.\n11\tThe distributed instruction integration pattern (\\S\\ref{sec:routing_synthesis}) implies that uniform pruning may be preferable to attention-score-based head selection for VLA compression.\n12\tExtended use cases are discussed in Appendix~\\ref{sec:extended_practical_implications}.\n13\tCode and data will be released upon acceptance.\n14\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EShKLG3tA1TJXTqiXRAZYQ","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"75cd33be-1689-4358-84be-a032362a8c7b","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:34:20.364Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Rkg8DMDRPNdfpWdUr96X1z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BHGaRsXaHPhXWzMVNHNV5z","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/docs/paper/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2923,"cache_read_input_tokens":23567,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2923,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":71,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EShKLG3tA1TJXTqiXRAZYQ","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"71761685-831d-4f96-89be-40b1ab2ad95b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FEcS3ZtKLMSxXVUJsNFr71","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LgJQjdAbFKM8Sgf8v3eXT3","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **novelty** and **related work**. You are conducting a blind peer review.\n\n## Venue Context: CoRL 2026\nCoRL (Conference on Robot Learning) is a top venue for robot learning research. Acceptance rate ~30%. Papers are evaluated on: significance/novelty of results (theoretical or empirical), relevance to robot learning, experimental rigor. CoRL strongly values behavioral/systems results over purely analytical/representational work. Scoring scale: 1 (strong reject) to 10 (strong accept), with 5 = marginally below threshold, 6 = marginally above.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Profiling Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Diagnostic Framework\"\n\nThe paper presents a diagnostic framework for token-level representational profiling of decoder-only VLAs, validated across 3 architectures (OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA) and 4 LIBERO suites. Framework combines factorial experimental designs, 6 probing methods, and counterfactual perturbation analysis.\n\n## Key Claims & Findings:\n1. **R² exit collapse** — consistent VLM→VLA transition marker (6/6 cells, Δ=0.156–0.455); absent in VLA→VLA (0/4 cells). NLP fine-tuning control ΔR²=0.000.\n2. **Counterfactual sensitivity** — all trained VLAs show d≥0.45. But 5/7 pairs use incommensurable metrics. Token overlap Spearman ρ=1.0 with Cohen's d → surface-level, not semantic. ΔMSE≈0 → no functional consequence.\n3. **L1 attention heads base-model-dependent** (D090) — previously the most visually striking result (0→9→24 gradient) shown to be base-model artifact, not training-induced. Phi-3-Vision: 32/32 heads untrained.\n4. **Metric dependence** — CKA reverses RSA ordering (Table 5).\n5. **Framework** positioned as primary surviving contribution after D090 self-critique.\n\n## Paper Structure:\n- Sec 1: Introduction — presents framework for profiling instruction routing in VLAs\n- Sec 2: Related Work — covers VLAs (RT-2, OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA), representational analysis (probing, RSA, CKA), instruction following in embodied agents (Grant et al. visual dominance, Haon et al. instruction underutilization)\n- Sec 3: Methods — framework overview (factorial design, 6 methods, CF protocol), architectures (3 VLAs × 4 LIBERO suites)\n- Sec 4: Experiments — token-level specialization (C1), encoder geometry, R² exit collapse, CF sensitivity, attention analysis, three-tier gradient, synthesis\n- Sec 5: Discussion — honest assessment, diagnostic utility question, metric dependence, missing behavioral grounding, framework value\n- Sec 6: Conclusion + Limitations\n\n## Key Context for Novelty Assessment:\n- The paper's own D090 finding removes its most visually striking result (L1 attention gradient)\n- The paper's own honest assessment (Sec 5) states findings are \"primarily descriptive\" and \"do not provide actionable insights for VLA design\"\n- Exit collapse is acknowledged as potentially \"trivially expected\" when adapting LM to produce actions\n- The paper includes a self-predicted score table (Table 19): mean 4.67/10, \"Likely Reject\"\n- CF sensitivity ρ=1.0 with token novelty = surface-level lexical sensitivity\n- ΔMSE≈0 from head ablation = no functional significance of instruction routing\n- DINO (no language, no action training) achieves R²=0.687 — challenges premise that instruction routing matters\n\n## Related Work Context:\n- Grant et al.: visual dominance in VLA behavior — instructions contribute minimally\n- Haon et al.: instruction underutilization in deployment\n- Hewitt & Liang (2019): information-computation dissociation — probe results don't prove functional use\n- Standard probing/RSA/CKA literature in NLP (Belinkov, Saphra, etc.)\n- No citation of VLA-specific representational analysis work (possibly because none exists — first-mover claim)\n\n## Your Task as R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is this framework? Are the findings genuinely new? Is the \"first systematic VLA profiling toolkit\" claim justified?\n2. **Related work adequacy**: Are comparisons to prior work complete? Missing references?\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper positioned correctly given its actual findings? Over-claiming or under-claiming?\n4. **Significance for CoRL**: Does this paper provide value to the robot learning community?\n5. **Self-critique implications**: The paper's own honest analysis (D090, ΔMSE≈0, metric dependence) essentially argues against its own contributions. Is this admirable transparency or a sign the paper isn't ready?\n6. **The premise question**: Given DINO R²=0.687 (vision alone is strong for actions), Grant et al. (visual dominance), and ΔMSE≈0 (ablating routing doesn't affect quality) — is studying instruction routing in VLAs the right research question?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, detailed)\n- Questions for authors (3-5)\n- Scores: Novelty (1-4), Significance (1-4), Overall (1-10), Confidence (1-5)\n- Recommendation: Strong Reject / Reject / Borderline / Weak Accept / Accept / Strong Accept\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":731,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1528,"cache_read_input_tokens":67684,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1528},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"ebca5eab-61f3-4396-b480-aae5419dafa4"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a739be6ffd9cb9af6","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgJQjdAbFKM8Sgf8v3eXT3","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **novelty** and **related work**. You are conducting a blind peer review.\n\n## Venue Context: CoRL 2026\nCoRL (Conference on Robot Learning) is a top venue for robot learning research. Acceptance rate ~30%. Papers are evaluated on: significance/novelty of results (theoretical or empirical), relevance to robot learning, experimental rigor. CoRL strongly values behavioral/systems results over purely analytical/representational work. Scoring scale: 1 (strong reject) to 10 (strong accept), with 5 = marginally below threshold, 6 = marginally above.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Profiling Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Diagnostic Framework\"\n\nThe paper presents a diagnostic framework for token-level representational profiling of decoder-only VLAs, validated across 3 architectures (OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA) and 4 LIBERO suites. Framework combines factorial experimental designs, 6 probing methods, and counterfactual perturbation analysis.\n\n## Key Claims & Findings:\n1. **R² exit collapse** — consistent VLM→VLA transition marker (6/6 cells, Δ=0.156–0.455); absent in VLA→VLA (0/4 cells). NLP fine-tuning control ΔR²=0.000.\n2. **Counterfactual sensitivity** — all trained VLAs show d≥0.45. But 5/7 pairs use incommensurable metrics. Token overlap Spearman ρ=1.0 with Cohen's d → surface-level, not semantic. ΔMSE≈0 → no functional consequence.\n3. **L1 attention heads base-model-dependent** (D090) — previously the most visually striking result (0→9→24 gradient) shown to be base-model artifact, not training-induced. Phi-3-Vision: 32/32 heads untrained.\n4. **Metric dependence** — CKA reverses RSA ordering (Table 5).\n5. **Framework** positioned as primary surviving contribution after D090 self-critique.\n\n## Paper Structure:\n- Sec 1: Introduction — presents framework for profiling instruction routing in VLAs\n- Sec 2: Related Work — covers VLAs (RT-2, OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA), representational analysis (probing, RSA, CKA), instruction following in embodied agents (Grant et al. visual dominance, Haon et al. instruction underutilization)\n- Sec 3: Methods — framework overview (factorial design, 6 methods, CF protocol), architectures (3 VLAs × 4 LIBERO suites)\n- Sec 4: Experiments — token-level specialization (C1), encoder geometry, R² exit collapse, CF sensitivity, attention analysis, three-tier gradient, synthesis\n- Sec 5: Discussion — honest assessment, diagnostic utility question, metric dependence, missing behavioral grounding, framework value\n- Sec 6: Conclusion + Limitations\n\n## Key Context for Novelty Assessment:\n- The paper's own D090 finding removes its most visually striking result (L1 attention gradient)\n- The paper's own honest assessment (Sec 5) states findings are \"primarily descriptive\" and \"do not provide actionable insights for VLA design\"\n- Exit collapse is acknowledged as potentially \"trivially expected\" when adapting LM to produce actions\n- The paper includes a self-predicted score table (Table 19): mean 4.67/10, \"Likely Reject\"\n- CF sensitivity ρ=1.0 with token novelty = surface-level lexical sensitivity\n- ΔMSE≈0 from head ablation = no functional significance of instruction routing\n- DINO (no language, no action training) achieves R²=0.687 — challenges premise that instruction routing matters\n\n## Related Work Context:\n- Grant et al.: visual dominance in VLA behavior — instructions contribute minimally\n- Haon et al.: instruction underutilization in deployment\n- Hewitt & Liang (2019): information-computation dissociation — probe results don't prove functional use\n- Standard probing/RSA/CKA literature in NLP (Belinkov, Saphra, etc.)\n- No citation of VLA-specific representational analysis work (possibly because none exists — first-mover claim)\n\n## Your Task as R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is this framework? Are the findings genuinely new? Is the \"first systematic VLA profiling toolkit\" claim justified?\n2. **Related work adequacy**: Are comparisons to prior work complete? Missing references?\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper positioned correctly given its actual findings? Over-claiming or under-claiming?\n4. **Significance for CoRL**: Does this paper provide value to the robot learning community?\n5. **Self-critique implications**: The paper's own honest analysis (D090, ΔMSE≈0, metric dependence) essentially argues against its own contributions. Is this admirable transparency or a sign the paper isn't ready?\n6. **The premise question**: Given DINO R²=0.687 (vision alone is strong for actions), Grant et al. (visual dominance), and ΔMSE≈0 (ablating routing doesn't affect quality) — is studying instruction routing in VLAs the right research question?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, detailed)\n- Questions for authors (3-5)\n- Scores: Novelty (1-4), Significance (1-4), Overall (1-10), Confidence (1-5)\n- Recommendation: Strong Reject / Reject / Borderline / Weak Accept / Accept / Strong Accept\n","uuid":"db7a21e1-6c0d-438f-8bcd-0adaef32f9a1","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867"}
