routing in VLAs\n- Sec 2: Related Work — covers VLAs (RT-2, OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA), representational analysis (probing, RSA, CKA), instruction following in embodied agents (Grant et al. visual dominance, Haon et al. instruction underutilization)\n- Sec 3: Methods — framework overview (factorial design, 6 methods, CF protocol), architectures (3 VLAs × 4 LIBERO suites)\n- Sec 4: Experiments — token-level specialization (C1), encoder geometry, R² exit collapse, CF sensitivity, attention analysis, three-tier gradient, synthesis\n- Sec 5: Discussion — honest assessment, diagnostic utility question, metric dependence, missing behavioral grounding, framework value\n- Sec 6: Conclusion + Limitations\n\n## Key Context for Novelty Assessment:\n- The paper's own D090 finding removes its most visually striking result (L1 attention gradient)\n- The paper's own honest assessment (Sec 5) states findings are \"primarily descriptive\" and \"do not provide actionable insights for VLA design\"\n- Exit collapse is acknowledged as potentially \"trivially expected\" when adapting LM to produce actions\n- The paper includes a self-predicted score table (Table 19): mean 4.67/10, \"Likely Reject\"\n- CF sensitivity ρ=1.0 with token novelty = surface-level lexical sensitivity\n- ΔMSE≈0 from head ablation = no functional significance of instruction routing\n- DINO (no language, no action training) achieves R²=0.687 — challenges premise that instruction routing matters\n\n## Related Work Context:\n- Grant et al.: visual dominance in VLA behavior — instructions contribute minimally\n- Haon et al.: instruction underutilization in deployment\n- Hewitt & Liang (2019): information-computation dissociation — probe results don't prove functional use\n- Standard probing/RSA/CKA literature in NLP (Belinkov, Saphra, etc.)\n- No citation of VLA-specific representational analysis work (possibly because none exists — first-mover claim)\n\n## Your Task as R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is this framework? Are the findings genuinely new? Is the \"first systematic VLA profiling toolkit\" claim justified?\n2. **Related work adequacy**: Are comparisons to prior work complete? Missing references?\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper positioned correctly given its actual findings? Over-claiming or under-claiming?\n4. **Significance for CoRL**: Does this paper provide value to the robot learning community?\n5. **Self-critique implications**: The paper's own honest analysis (D090, ΔMSE≈0, metric dependence) essentially argues against its own contributions. Is this admirable transparency or a sign the paper isn't ready?\n6. **The premise question**: Given DINO R²=0.687 (vision alone is strong for actions), Grant et al. (visual dominance), and ΔMSE≈0 (ablating routing doesn't affect quality) — is studying instruction routing in VLAs the right research question?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, detailed)\n- Questions for authors (3-5)\n- Scores: Novelty (1-4), Significance (1-4), Overall (1-10), Confidence (1-5)\n- Recommendation: Strong Reject / Reject / Borderline / Weak Accept / Accept / Strong Accept\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgJQjdAbFKM8Sgf8v3eXT3","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"0213de00-8a7f-40bd-8104-c20444429530","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:34:21.064Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BHGaRsXaHPhXWzMVNHNV5z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tVision-language-action models (VLAs) fine-tune pretrained language models on robot demonstrations to produce actions from visual observations and language instructions~\\cite{brohan2023_rt2,kim2024_openvla}, but which of the resulting representational changes reflect action training and which are inherited from the pretrained backbone?\n5\tIn these decoder-only architectures, image tokens precede instruction tokens, and the causal attention mask restricts multimodal integration to a single readout token~\\cite{wang2025seeing,pei2025rethinking}; how representations at this critical position are reshaped by action fine-tuning remains unknown.\n6\t\n7\tBehavioral studies show that VLAs exhibit visual dominance~\\cite{grant2026} and underweight instructions~\\cite{haon2025}, but existing representational analyses target single architectures.\n8\tNo prior work tests whether instruction routing mechanisms are consistent across backbone families or decomposes training-associated effects from inherited base-model priors.\n9\t\n10\tWe address this gap with a cross-architecture analysis of three VLAs (OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024_openvla}, TracVLA~\\cite{zheng2024tracevla}, and NORA~\\cite{hung2025nora}) across four LIBERO~\\cite{liu2023_libero} task suites (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}).\n11\tTracVLA and NORA baselines are pure VLMs ($N = 2$ independent VLM-to-VLA transitions); OpenVLA's baseline is the OXE-pretrained~\\cite{padalkar2023_oxe} VLA (VLA-to-VLA transition).\n12\t\n13\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n14\t\\centering\n15\t\\includegraphics[width=0.88\\linewidth]{figures/fig_overview.pdf}\n16\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n17\t\\caption{\\textbf{Analysis framework.} We analyze hidden states at image-token averages and the readout token via layer-wise action probes, counterfactual instruction perturbations, and L1 attention profiling to distinguish training-associated changes from inherited backbone priors.}\n18\t\\label{fig:overview}\n19\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n20\t\\end{figure}\n21\t\n22\tOur analysis reveals three findings (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}): (1)~VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning is associated with substantial $R^2$ exit collapse ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$), whereas VLA-to-VLA fine-tuning and reference models show only small fluctuations ($\\Delta R^2 < 0.10$), with three NLP controls (all $\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) confirming transition-type specificity; (2)~all trained VLAs exhibit medium-to-large counterfactual sensitivity ($d = 0.45$--$1.52$); and (3)~L1 instruction attention reflects base-model priors, not VLA training (\\S\\ref{sec:r2_collapse}--\\ref{sec:l1_priors}).\n23\tWhile concurrent work evaluates VLA instruction-following behaviorally~\\cite{fang2026when,swann2026}, we provide complementary representational analysis of how instruction information is routed internally.\n24\tOur contributions are:\n25\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep]\n26\t\\item A cross-architecture representational analysis of three VLAs, combining trained/reference controls with token-level probing across a $3 \\times 4$ architecture-suite matrix.\n27\t\\item Evidence that $R^2$ exit collapse is associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions ($\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ across two architectures) but not with VLA-to-VLA fine-tuning or NLP instruction tuning of the same backbones.\n28\t\\item A decomposition of VLA instruction routing into training-associated effects and inherited base-model priors, based on cross-architecture trained/reference contrasts.\n29\t\\end{enumerate}\n30\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EShKLG3tA1TJXTqiXRAZYQ","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"00ad101b-fc6a-4b4c-bce6-2c0519ce75a2","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:34:20.817Z"}
