{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CMZMH5mCFZeC6HLbZQBLXH","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\n201\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n202\t\\centering\n203\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_specificity_bootstrap_30seeds.pdf}\n204\t\\caption{$R^2$ increase from ablating 24/32 L1 heads. Gray bars: 30 random subsets; red line: dominant-24 heads ($+0.030$). Random subsets yield larger gains (mean $+0.071 \\pm 0.013$; $z = -3.11$, percentile $= 0\\%$), consistent with graded attention specialization across L1 heads.}\n205\t\\label{fig:random_subset_ablation}\n206\t\\end{figure}\n207\t\n208\t\\subsection{Action Trajectory Analysis Details}\n209\t\\label{sec:action_trajectory}\n210\t\n211\tTable~\\ref{tab:action_shift_perdim} reports per-dimension mean absolute shift for instruction-dominant (24 heads) and control (8 heads) L1 ablations.\n212\tBoth conditions produce negligible shifts on translational dimensions ($dx$, $dy$, $dz$ $< 0.01$) and small rotational shifts ($rx$, $ry$, $rz$ $< 0.04$).\n213\tThe gripper dimension absorbs the majority of the effect: instruction-head ablation shifts gripper predictions by $0.291$ (control: $0.141$), consistent with L1 attention influencing discrete open/close decisions.\n214\t\n215\tPer-task Cohen's $d$ values for instruction-head ablation are $0.43$, $0.69$, $0.70$, $0.70$, and $0.73$ (mean $0.65$), with all tasks significant ($p < 10^{-10}$).\n216\tToken-level KL divergence between original and ablated action distributions is $5.08$ for instruction heads and $1.69$ for control heads.\n217\t\n218\t\\paragraph{Trajectory-level aggregation.}\n219\tAggregating per-demo mean actions to the trajectory level reveals that token-level $d$ values are conservative: within-trajectory averaging reduces noise, yielding ${\\sim}4\\times$ larger effect sizes (mean token-to-trajectory ratio $0.245$).\n220\tInstruction-head ablation ($N = 50$: 5 tasks $\\times$ 10 demos) yields trajectory $d = 2.943$ (95\\% CI $[2.469, 3.707]$); bottom-24 non-instruction heads ($N = 30$) yield $d = 2.347$ (CI $[1.854, 3.221]$).\n221\tAll 14 conditions produce trajectory $d > 2.0$ with CI lower bounds $> 1.7$.\n222\tCounterfactual trajectory-level $d$ (10 LIBERO-Goal tasks, $N = 100$): wrong $3.798$ (CI $[3.194, 4.938]$), empty $3.438$ ($[2.921, 4.448]$), shuffled $2.595$ ($[2.028, 3.791]$), paraphrased $2.131$ ($[1.740, 2.819]$).\n223\tToken-level $d$ underestimates trajectory-level effects by ${\\times}3.1$--$4.5$, consistent with within-trajectory averaging reducing noise.\n224\tFine-grained ordering is not reliably distinguished at trajectory level ($N = 100$); token-level comparisons ($N {\\sim} 5{,}000$) provide higher resolution for ordering claims.\n225\t\n226\t\\paragraph{Ground-truth action error.}\n227\tAblation redirects model outputs ($d = 0.65$) but does not systematically increase error relative to expert demonstrations: original MSE $= 0.498$; top-24 instruction-head ablation MSE $= 0.497$ ($\\Delta = -0.001$, Wilcoxon $p = 0.52$, n.s.); bottom-24 non-instruction heads MSE $= 0.506$ ($\\Delta = +0.008$).\n228\tChanges concentrate on the gripper dimension; translation and rotation dimensions are negligibly affected.\n229\tPer-task breakdown shows all $|\\Delta\\text{MSE}| < 3\\%$ of baseline with all $p > 0.27$; the near-zero aggregate $\\Delta$ reflects uniformly small per-task effects rather than cancellation across tasks.\n230\tThis is consistent with the information-computation dissociation: instruction-dominant heads control instruction-specific direction selection rather than action precision.\n231\t\n232\tTable~\\ref{tab:perhead_bootstrap} reports per-head bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples).\n233\tIndividual control heads produce ${\\sim}1.9\\times$ stronger effects ($d = 0.051$) than instruction heads ($d = 0.027$, $p = 0.0018$), consistent across all 5 tasks.\n234\tThe 24 instruction heads nonetheless produce a larger aggregate perturbation ($d = 0.65$ vs.\\ $0.41$), consistent with collective coordination rather than individual strength.\n235\t\n236\t\\begin{table}[h]\n237\t\\centering\n238\t\\caption{Per-head bootstrap analysis of L1 ablation effects. Instruction heads are individually weaker but collectively stronger, consistent with coordinated attention redistribution.}\n239\t\\label{tab:perhead_bootstrap}\n240\t\\small\n241\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n242\t\\toprule\n243\tGroup & $N$ heads & Collective $d$ & Per-head $d$ [95\\% CI] \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\tInstruction-dominant & 24 & 0.65 & 0.027 [0.026, 0.029] \\\\\n246\tControl & 8 & 0.41 & 0.051 [0.048, 0.055] \\\\\n247\t\\midrule\n248\tDifference & --- & --- & 0.024, $p = 0.0018$ \\\\\n249\t\\bottomrule\n250\t\\end{tabular}\n251\t\\end{table}\n252\t\n253\tInstruction-head ablation produces more uniform perturbation across action dimensions (CV${}=1.30$ vs.\\ control CV${}=2.74$, permutation $p = 0.016$), consistent with structured, language-conditioned computation rather than diffuse noise.\n254\t\n255\tTable~\\ref{tab:dose_response} and Figure~\\ref{fig:dose_response} report the dose-response gradient for instruction-head ablation at L1.\n256\tTrajectory divergence increases monotonically from $d = 0.482$ (4 heads) to $d = 0.639$ (32 heads), with steep rise through 12 heads (slope $\\Delta d / \\Delta 4 \\approx 0.026$), a plateau at 16--20 heads ($\\Delta d = 0.001$), and slow increase at 20--32.\n257\t\n258\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n259\t\\centering\n260\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}\n261\t\\caption{Dose-response gradient: trajectory divergence (Cohen's $d$) as a function of ablated instruction-dominant heads at L1. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples). Dashed red: control ablation (8 non-instruction heads, $d = 0.41$). Steep rise through 12 heads, plateau at 16--20, diminishing returns beyond.}\n262\t\\label{fig:dose_response}\n263\t\\end{figure}\n264\t\n265\t\\begin{table}[h]\n266\t\\centering\n267\t\\caption{Dose-response data for L1 instruction-head ablation. Cohen's $d$ vs.\\ original action sequence; 95\\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples).}\n268\t\\label{tab:dose_response}\n269\t\\small\n270\t\\begin{tabular}{rcc}\n271\t\\toprule\n272\tHeads ablated & Cohen's $d$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n273\t\\midrule\n274\t4 & 0.482 & [0.449, 0.511] \\\\\n275\t8 & 0.508 & [0.479, 0.541] \\\\\n276\t12 & 0.560 & [0.529, 0.593] \\\\\n277\t16 & 0.592 & [0.559, 0.625] \\\\\n278\t20 & 0.593 & [0.559, 0.627] \\\\\n279\t24 & 0.611 & [0.578, 0.645] \\\\\n280\t28 & 0.619 & [0.587, 0.654] \\\\\n281\t32 & 0.639 & [0.603, 0.671] \\\\\n282\t\\bottomrule\n283\t\\end{tabular}\n284\t\\end{table}\n285\t\n286\t\\begin{table}[h]\n287\t\\centering\n288\t\\caption{Counterfactual instruction analysis across all 10 LIBERO-Goal tasks. Jaccard: token overlap with original instruction; Cohen's $d$: mean across 10 tasks. ``Wrong'' slightly exceeds ``empty'' ($d = 1.182$ vs.\\ $1.170$); all four conditions produce large effects ($d \\geq 1.0$). L1 ablation included for comparison.}\n289\t\\small\n290\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n291\t\\toprule\n292\tCondition & Jaccard & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\tEmpty (no instruction) & 0.000 & 1.170 \\\\\n295\tWrong (different task) & 0.174 & 1.182 \\\\\n296\tShuffled (random order) & 1.000 & 1.096 \\\\\n297\tParaphrased & 0.533 & 0.996 \\\\\n298\t\\midrule\n299\tL1 ablation (24 heads) & --- & 0.650 \\\\\n300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[h]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\caption{Mean absolute action shift per dimension under L1 head ablation. Instruction: 24 dominant heads; Control: 8 remaining heads. Gripper shift dominates both conditions.}\n307\t\\label{tab:action_shift_perdim}\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n310\t\\toprule\n311\tCondition & $dx$ & $dy$ & $dz$ & $rx$ & $ry$ & $rz$ & Gripper \\\\\n312\t\\midrule\n313\tInstruction (24) & 0.002 & 0.005 & 0.008 & 0.021 & 0.031 & 0.034 & 0.291 \\\\\n314\tControl (8) & 0.000 & 0.001 & 0.002 & 0.008 & 0.014 & 0.018 & 0.141 \\\\\n315\t\\bottomrule\n316\t\\end{tabular}\n317\t\\end{table}\n318\t\n319\t\\subsection{Pretrained LLM Representation Quality Under Visual Input}\n320\tA potential concern with the three-condition comparison (\\S\\ref{sec:arch_data}) is that the pretrained Llama-2 backbone never encountered projected visual tokens during pretraining, so its representations of such input could be degenerate.\n321\tWe evaluate three diagnostics to assess representation quality.\n322\tActivation magnitude grows from $16.5$ (L0) to $90.2$ (L31) for Llama-2 base, compared to $16.5 \\to 51.7$ for the VLA-trained model; the pretrained LLM produces larger but non-exploding activations with no evidence of collapse.\n323\tPer-layer cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers decreases progressively from $1.0$ to $\\approx 0.28$ at L25, consistent with gradual differentiation rather than catastrophic out-of-distribution behavior.\n324\tTop-10 principal component variance explained remains stable across all layers ($0.59$--$0.74$), with no low-rank collapse.\n325\tThese diagnostics indicate that the pretrained LLM produces structured, full-rank representations of visual input despite never encountering projected visual tokens during pretraining (Figure~\\ref{fig:ood_diagnostics}). These results support the validity of the three-condition comparison.\n326\t\n327\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n328\t\\centering\n329\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_ood_diagnostics.pdf}\n330\t\\caption{Pretrained LLM representation diagnostics under visual input. Left: activation magnitude across layers (Llama-2 base vs.\\ VLA-trained). Center: cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers. Right: top-10 PC variance explained. All three metrics indicate structured, non-degenerate representations.}\n331\t\\label{fig:ood_diagnostics}\n332\t\\end{figure}\n333\t\n334\t\\subsection{Probe Architecture Sensitivity}\n335\t\\label{sec:linear_probe}\n336\t\n337\tTo rule out that our findings depend on MLP probe capacity ($N/\\text{params} \\approx 0.06$, overparameterized), we replicate the per-layer analysis with a linear probe ($4{,}096 \\to 7$, $N/\\text{params} \\approx 2.2$).\n338\tThe inverted-U pattern reproduces: trained image\\_mean peaks at L14 ($R^2 = 0.565$), while the reference declines monotonically from L0 ($R^2 = 0.517$).\n339\tThe trained--reference gap is larger under the linear probe (L14: $\\Delta = +0.324$) than the MLP probe (L8: $\\Delta = +0.015$); trained representations are thus more probe-decodable for action information.\n340\tReference deep layers collapse entirely under the linear probe (L25: $R^2 = -0.298$); MLP nonlinearity partially rescues these degraded representations.\n341\tFor readout tokens, the linear probe also corroborates the inverted-U (peak L13, $R^2 = 0.596$), with the same peak layer as the MLP probe and a trained-vs-reference gap of $\\Delta = 0.103$ ($p < 0.001$).\n342\tThese results indicate that our qualitative findings reflect representational structure rather than probe capacity artifacts (Figure~\\ref{fig:linear_vs_mlp}).\n343\t\n344\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n345\t\\centering\n346\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_linear_vs_mlp_probe.pdf}\n347\t\\caption{Per-layer action probe $R^2$ for linear (dashed) and MLP (solid) probes across trained (blue) and reference (red) conditions. The inverted-U reproduces under both probe architectures; the trained--reference gap is larger for linear probes.}\n348\t\\label{fig:linear_vs_mlp}\n349\t\\end{figure}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Functional Probe Comparison}\n352\tFigure~\\ref{fig:probe_bars} compares functional probe $R^2$ across representation sources at each source's best layer.\n353\t\n354\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n355\t\\centering\n356\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig4.pdf}\n357\t\\caption{Functional probe $R^2$ for action prediction (best layer per source). Trained readout ($R^2{=}0.678$, L13) and trained image\\_mean ($0.655$, L8) exceed the projector baseline ($0.630$); the inverted-U extends to functional capacity.}\n358\t\\label{fig:probe_bars}\n359\t\\end{figure}\n360\t\n361\t\\subsection{Training Effect and Instruction Invariance}\n362\tFigure~\\ref{fig:factorial} shows trained vs.\\ reference RSA profiles and instruction invariance.\n363\tInstruction invariance holds across all conditions: maximum $|\\Delta| < 0.006$, so action geometry is visually grounded and not modulated by language instructions.\n364\t\n365\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n366\t\\centering\n367\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig5.pdf}\n368\t\\caption{\\textbf{(a)}~Trained vs.\\ reference image\\_mean RSA profiles. Training shifts the peak from L0 ($0.612$) to L12 ($0.628$), yielding a modest best-vs-best gain ($\\Delta{=}+0.017$, $3\\%$). \\textbf{(b)}~Instruction invariance: all conditions within $|\\Delta|{<}0.006$; action geometry is visually grounded.}\n369\t\\label{fig:factorial}\n370\t\\end{figure}\n371\t\n372\t\\subsection{Additional RSA Values}\n373\tDINOv2 \\texttt{patch\\_mean} achieves RSA $\\rho = 0.631$, between the \\texttt{cls\\_token} ($0.692$) and the fused DinoSigLIP ($0.634$).\n374\tVLA reference readout peaks at L1 ($\\rho = 0.626$).\n375\t\n376\t\\subsection{Cross-Suite Validation within LIBERO: Spatial}\n377\t\\label{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}\n378\t\n379\tTo assess generalization beyond in-distribution tasks, we repeat the core RSA and probe analyses on LIBERO-Spatial (10 out-of-distribution tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations each, requiring spatial reasoning).\n380\tFigure~\\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} shows per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles for both LIBERO-Goal and LIBERO-Spatial side by side.\n381\t\n382\tThe inverted-U profile replicates on LIBERO-Spatial: trained image\\_mean RSA peaks at L14 ($\\rho = 0.691$) and $R^2$ at L13 ($0.696$), with peaks shifting ${\\sim}2$ layers deeper than LIBERO-Goal (RSA L12, $R^2$ L8--L10), consistent with increased task difficulty requiring deeper processing.\n383\tTraining produces a significant geometric gain (best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, 95\\% CI $[0.012, 0.036]$, $p < 0.001$), larger than the LIBERO-Goal effect ($\\Delta = +0.017$, CI includes zero).\n384\t\n385\tTwo notable differences are observed.\n386\tFirst, the absolute RSA values are higher on Spatial ($0.691$ vs.\\ $0.628$), possibly reflecting greater action diversity in spatial tasks that produces a more discriminative action RDM.\n387\tSecond, RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate: readout RSA peaks at L15 while $R^2$ peaks at L21 (6-layer gap), contrasting with the near-alignment on Goal ($|\\Delta_\\text{layer}| = 1$).\n388\tThis suggests that peak convergence is benchmark-specific rather than a general consequence of training.\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig7.pdf}\n393\t\\caption{Cross-suite validation: per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles on LIBERO-Goal (left) vs.\\ LIBERO-Spatial (right). The inverted-U replicates across suites. LIBERO-Spatial shows a significant training effect (RSA best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, $p < 0.001$) and deeper peak layers (RSA L14, $R^2$ L13), but RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate (6-layer gap).}\n394\t\\label{fig:cross_benchmark}\n395\t\\end{figure}\n396\t\n397\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Suite Counterfactual Replication}\n398\tTable~\\ref{tab:counterfactual_spatial} reports counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks).\n399\tThe ordering differs from LIBERO-Goal: empty $>$ shuffled $>$ wrong $>$ paraphrased (vs.\\ wrong $>$ empty $>$ shuffled $>$ paraphrased on Goal).\n400\tAll global effect sizes exceed $d = 0.8$ (per-task wrong minimum is $0.591$).\n401\tSpatial instructions differ only in prepositions (e.g., ``on the plate'' vs.\\ ``in the drawer''); the wrong-task $d = 0.885$ suggests that instruction-dominant heads encode fine-grained spatial relations, not merely task identity.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[h]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\caption{Counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks). Ordering: empty $>$ shuffled $>$ wrong $>$ paraphrased, differing from Goal. All global $d > 0.8$.}\n406\t\\label{tab:counterfactual_spatial}\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\tCondition & Jaccard & Cohen's $d$ & CV \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tEmpty (no instruction) & 0.000 & 1.173 & 0.087 \\\\\n413\tWrong (different task) & 0.174 & 0.885 & 0.272 \\\\\n414\tShuffled (random order) & 1.000 & 0.979 & 0.162 \\\\\n415\tParaphrased & 0.533 & 0.807 & 0.179 \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Probe on LIBERO-Spatial}\n421\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial_cross_instruction} reports cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial.\n422\tThe core pattern from LIBERO-Goal replicates: image\\_mean remains at chance (${\\sim}10\\%$) across all layers and conditions, while the trained readout token saturates to $100\\%$ by L4.\n423\tThe reference readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal ($75.4\\%$ at L31 vs.\\ $82\\%$), consistent with greater task difficulty.\n424\t\n425\t\\begin{table}[h]\n426\t\\centering\n427\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial. Trained readout achieves $100\\%$ from L4; image\\_mean remains at chance. Reference readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal (cf.\\ \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}).}\n428\t\\label{tab:spatial_cross_instruction}\n429\t\\small\n430\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n431\t\\toprule\n432\tToken & Condition & L1 & L4 & L8 & L16 & L31 \\\\\n433\t\\midrule\n434\treadout & Trained & 98.9\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% \\\\\n435\treadout & Reference & 99.5\\% & 98.3\\% & 85.7\\% & 68.3\\% & 75.4\\% \\\\\n436\timage\\_mean & Trained & 10.2\\% & 10.0\\% & 9.9\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.1\\% \\\\\n437\timage\\_mean & Reference & 10.1\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.2\\% \\\\\n438\t\\bottomrule\n439\t\\end{tabular}\n440\t\\end{table}\n441\t\n442\t\\subsection{Robustness Analyses}\n443\t\\subsubsection{Distance Metric Invariance}\n444\tThe inverted-U profile is robust to distance metric choice: cosine action distance yields identical peak layers (L12) and Pearson correlation $r > 0.989$ with the Euclidean profile reported in the main text (Table~\\ref{tab:metric_invariance}).\n445\t\n446\t\\begin{table}[h]\n447\t\\centering\n448\t\\caption{Peak layer comparison between Euclidean and cosine action distances. Profile $r$ denotes Pearson correlation across all 32 layers.}\n449\t\\label{tab:metric_invariance}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EShKLG3tA1TJXTqiXRAZYQ","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"d285a486-4d27-45e1-848a-82573988120c","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:34:32.950Z"}
