{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018NDEjn8geZ4ENvrPeqmKSs","type":"tool_result","content":"700\t\\centering\n701\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_probe_conditions.pdf}\n702\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy at the readout token across all 32 LLM layers. VLA-trained achieves $99.6\\%$ at L1, saturating to $100\\%$. Llama-2 base starts at $80.2\\%$, reaching $100\\%$ by L5. Reference starts at $100\\%$ but decays to $82\\%$ by L31.}\n703\t\\end{figure}\n704\t\n705\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Architecture Probe and Attention Profile}\n706\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_probe} reports cross-instruction probe accuracy and L1 instruction-dominant head counts across six conditions spanning two architectures.\n707\tThe probe-attention dissociation at L1 is notable: TracVLA training slightly \\emph{reduces} instruction-dominant heads ($32 \\to 27$--$30/32$) while \\emph{decreasing} probe accuracy ($98.1\\% \\to 83.6\\%$), consistent with representational reorganization rather than linear separability.\n708\t\n709\t\\begin{table}[h]\n710\t\\centering\n711\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way probe accuracy and L1 instruction-dominant heads across six conditions.\n712\tProbe accuracy and attention head count are dissociated at L1: TracVLA training slightly reduces heads ($32 \\to 27$--$30/32$) while decreasing probe accuracy ($98.1\\% \\to 83.6\\%$), paralleling the information-computation dissociation observed in OpenVLA.\n713\tImage\\_mean remains at chance ($10\\%$) in all conditions (omitted).}\n714\t\\label{tab:cross_arch_probe}\n715\t\\small\n716\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n717\t\\toprule\n718\tCondition & L1 heads & L1 & L2+ & L31 & Mode \\\\\n719\t\\midrule\n720\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{OpenVLA (Llama-2 7B)}} \\\\\n721\t\\quad Random init & 0/32 & 100.0\\% & decays & 82.0\\% & incid.+forget \\\\\n722\t\\quad Llama-2 base & 9/32 & 80.2\\% & L5: 100\\% & 100.0\\% & gradual+stable \\\\\n723\t\\quad VLA trained & 24/32 & 99.6\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & immed.+stable \\\\\n724\t\\quad LIBERO-finetuned & 22/32 & 99.7\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & immed.+stable \\\\\n725\t\\midrule\n726\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{TraceVLA (Phi-3-V 4B)}} \\\\\n727\t\\quad Phi-3-Vision & 32/32 & 98.1\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & inherited+stable \\\\\n728\t\\quad VLA trained & 27--30/32 & 83.6\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & reorg.+stable \\\\\n729\t\\bottomrule\n730\t\\end{tabular}\n731\t\\end{table}\n732\t\n733\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n734\t\\centering\n735\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cross_architecture_attention.pdf}\n736\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads across layers for two architectures (OpenVLA / TraceVLA), each comparing pre-VLA and VLA-trained conditions. Despite opposite starting points---OpenVLA (Llama-2) begins with sparse attention while TraceVLA (Phi-3-V) begins with distributed attention---VLA training drives both to entry-exit concentration (L1 saturation, mid-layer suppression, L29--L31 re-engagement).}\n737\t\\end{figure}\n738\t\n739\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Architecture L1 Instruction Dominance}\n740\tFigure~\\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1_bar} complements the main-text summary (Figure~\\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1}) by showing the fraction of instruction-dominant heads at L1 across all three architectures in a single comparison. The trained/reference fractions are comparable within each architecture (OpenVLA: 23/32 vs.\\ 26/32; TracVLA: 27/32 vs.\\ 32/32; NORA: 13/16 vs.\\ 13/16), consistent with L1 instruction attention being largely inherited from the pretrained backbone rather than acquired during VLA fine-tuning.\n741\t\n742\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n743\t\\centering\n744\t\\includegraphics[width=0.65\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cross_arch_l1.pdf}\n745\t\\caption{\\textbf{L1 instruction-dominant head fractions across three architectures.} Trained and reference conditions show comparable fractions in all three VLAs, indicating that L1 instruction attention largely pre-exists in the pretrained backbone.}\n746\t\\label{fig:cross_arch_l1_bar}\n747\t\\end{figure}\n748\t\n749\t\\subsubsection{L1 Attention Pattern Analysis}\n750\tFigure~\\ref{fig:attention_appendix} shows the per-head L1 attention distribution at the readout token.\n751\tThe three-level gradient (randomly initialized 0/32, Llama-2 base 9/32, VLA-trained 24/32 instruction-dominant heads) shows that language pretraining seeds partial instruction attention and VLA fine-tuning shifts it further toward instruction dominance.\n752\t\n753\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n754\t\\centering\n755\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_stacked_bar.pdf}\n756\t\\caption{L1 attention from the readout token across three conditions, per head (sorted by instruction attention fraction). VLA-trained: 24/32 instruction-dominant heads ($57.7\\%$ text attention). Llama-2 base: 9/32 heads ($36.5\\%$). Randomly initialized: 0/32 heads ($11.7\\%$). See Table~\\ref{tab:multilayer_attention} for quantitative comparison across layers.}\n757\t\\label{fig:attention_appendix}\n758\t\\end{figure}\n759\t\n760\t\\subsubsection{Multi-Layer Attention Patterns}\n761\tTable~\\ref{tab:multilayer_attention} shows a 4-layer subset of the full 32-layer attention profile.\n762\tThe full profile (Figure~\\ref{fig:multilayer_attention}) reveals three qualitatively distinct patterns: the randomly initialized model maintains 0 instruction-dominant heads at all 32 layers; Llama-2 base shows a mid-depth peak (L9--L16: 15--22 heads), consistent with typical autoregressive LM processing depth; VLA fine-tuning produces an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1: 21--24 heads, L31: 18 heads, intermediate layers 0--11).\n763\t\n764\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n765\t\\centering\n766\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_multi_layer_attn.pdf}\n767\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and mean text attention fraction across layers for three conditions. VLA-trained shows elevated values at L1 and L31; Llama-2 base peaks at L16; randomly initialized remains near zero throughout.}\n768\t\\label{fig:multilayer_attention}\n769\t\\end{figure}\n770\t\n771\t\\begin{table}[h]\n772\t\\centering\n773\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and text attention fraction at four sampled layers (subset of the 32-layer profile in Figure~\\ref{fig:multilayer_attention}). VLA-trained shows entry-exit concentration (L1, L31); Llama-2 base peaks at mid-depth (L16); randomly initialized shows no instruction attention. Head counts from the 32-layer bulk extraction may differ slightly from dedicated per-layer analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}) due to threshold boundary effects.}\n774\t\\label{tab:multilayer_attention}\n775\t\\small\n776\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n777\t\\toprule\n778\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{VLA Trained} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Llama-2 base} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Rand.\\ Init} \\\\\n779\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5} \\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\n780\tLayer & Heads & Text\\% & Heads & Text\\% & Heads & Text\\% \\\\\n781\t\\midrule\n782\tL1 & \\textbf{24/32} & 57.7 & 9/32 & 36.5 & 0/32 & 11.7 \\\\\n783\tL8 & 4/32 & 46.8 & 8/32 & 48.8 & 0/32 & 7.9 \\\\\n784\tL16 & 10/32 & 48.4 & \\textbf{25/32} & 60.2 & 0/32 & 9.2 \\\\\n785\tL31 & \\textbf{18/32} & 56.3 & 8/32 & 34.9 & 0/32 & 7.6 \\\\\n786\t\\bottomrule\n787\t\\end{tabular}\n788\t\\end{table}\n789\t\n790\t\\subsubsection{Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}\n791\t\\label{sec:threshold_sensitivity}\n792\t\n793\tThe main text defines instruction-dominant heads as those allocating $>50\\%$ attention to instruction tokens.\n794\tTable~\\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity} shows that the three-condition gradient (randomly initialized $<$ Llama-2 base $<$ VLA-trained) holds across all thresholds from 30\\% to 70\\%; the 50\\% threshold is not a boundary-sensitive choice.\n795\t\n796\t\\begin{table}[h]\n797\t\\centering\n798\t\\caption{Number of instruction-dominant heads at L1 under varying attention thresholds. The three-condition ordering is stable across all thresholds.}\n799\t\\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}\n800\t\\small\n801\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n802\t\\toprule\n803\tThreshold & VLA Trained & Llama-2 base & Rand.\\ Init \\\\\n804\t\\midrule\n805\t30\\% & 32 & 13 & 0 \\\\\n806\t40\\% & 31 & 10 & 0 \\\\\n807\t50\\% & 24 & 9 & 0 \\\\\n808\t60\\% & 9 & 4 & 0 \\\\\n809\t70\\% & 5 & 3 & 0 \\\\\n810\t\\bottomrule\n811\t\\end{tabular}\n812\t\\end{table}\n813\t\n814\t\\subsection{L1 Attention Decomposition by Head Group}\n815\t\\label{sec:attention_decomp}\n816\t\n817\tTable~\\ref{tab:attention_decomp} decomposes L1 attention mass at the readout token into three token categories for instruction-dominant and control head groups.\n818\t\n819\t\\begin{table}[h]\n820\t\\centering\n821\t\\caption{Attention mass allocation at Layer 1, averaged across 5 samples. Instruction-dominant heads (defined as $>50\\%$ attention to instruction tokens) allocate 17.5 percentage points more attention to instruction tokens than control heads, with corresponding reductions in vision ($-8.3$pp) and BOS/sink ($-9.2$pp) attention.}\n822\t\\label{tab:attention_decomp}\n823\t\\small\n824\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n825\t\\toprule\n826\tHead Group & $N$ & Instruction & Vision & BOS/Other \\\\\n827\t\\midrule\n828\tInstruction-dominant & 24 & 62.4\\% & 18.8\\% & 18.8\\% \\\\\n829\tControl & 8 & 44.9\\% & 27.1\\% & 28.0\\% \\\\\n830\t\\midrule\n831\t$\\Delta$ & --- & $+$17.5pp & $-$8.3pp & $-$9.2pp \\\\\n832\t\\bottomrule\n833\t\\end{tabular}\n834\t\\end{table}\n835\t\n836\tHead-level instruction attention entropy reaches $99.5\\%$ of the uniform maximum; attention is broadly distributed rather than concentrated in a few heads.\n837\t\n838\t\\paragraph{Per-token attention normalization.}\n839\tThe $57.7\\%$ aggregate text attention in instruction-dominant heads partly reflects token-count imbalance (22 instruction vs.\\ 256 image tokens).\n840\tPer-token normalization reveals a $38.2\\times$ ratio: each instruction token receives $38.2\\times$ the attention of each image token in trained instruction-dominant heads, compared to only $1.5\\times$ in randomly initialized heads (a $25.5\\times$ increase from training).\n841\tInstruction-dominant heads also show lower attention entropy ($0.465$) than non-dominant heads ($0.569$), indicating more concentrated attention distributions.\n842\t\n843\t\\subsection{Per-Dimension Action MAE Under Head Ablation}\n844\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_dim_mae} extends Table~\\ref{tab:action_shift_perdim} with full per-dimension control-head data and instruction-to-control MAE ratios.\n845\t\n846\t\\begin{table}[h]\n847\t\\centering\n848\t\\caption{Mean absolute error per action dimension under L1 head ablation. Translation dimensions ($dx$/$dy$/$dz$) show 3--5$\\times$ larger instruction-to-control ratio than rotation dimensions (1.9--2.6$\\times$); instruction-dominant heads have disproportionate influence on positional control. Gripper (2.07$\\times$) falls between the two groups.}\n849\t\\label{tab:per_dim_mae}\n850\t\\small\n851\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n852\t\\toprule\n853\tDimension & Instr.\\ 24h MAE & Control 8h MAE & Ratio \\\\\n854\t\\midrule\n855\t$dx$ & 0.00189 & 0.00037 & 5.09$\\times$ \\\\\n856\t$dy$ & 0.00531 & 0.00125 & 4.26$\\times$ \\\\\n857\t$dz$ & 0.00762 & 0.00243 & 3.14$\\times$ \\\\\n858\t$roll$ & 0.02059 & 0.00785 & 2.62$\\times$ \\\\\n859\t$pitch$ & 0.03071 & 0.01387 & 2.21$\\times$ \\\\\n860\t$yaw$ & 0.03388 & 0.01783 & 1.90$\\times$ \\\\\n861\t$gripper$ & 0.29072 & 0.14072 & 2.07$\\times$ \\\\\n862\t\\bottomrule\n863\t\\end{tabular}\n864\t\\end{table}\n865\t\n866\t\\subsection{Per-Task $R^2$ Distribution Under L1 Ablation}\n867\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_task_r2} reports per-task action probe $R^2$ at L1 under baseline and 24-head ablation conditions (MLP probe, 5 demonstrations per task).\n868\t\n869\t\\begin{table}[h]\n870\t\\centering\n871\t\\caption{Per-task action probe $R^2$ at L1 (MLP probe, 5 demos/task). All 10 tasks show positive ablation effect ($\\Delta > 0$): instruction-head ablation uniformly increases action decodability at the entry layer. Mean $\\Delta R^2 = +0.107$; the 50-demo setting (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}) yields a consistent $+0.059$.}\n872\t\\label{tab:per_task_r2}\n873\t\\small\n874\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n875\t\\toprule\n876\tTask & Baseline $R^2$ & Ablated $R^2$ & $\\Delta R^2$ \\\\\n877\t\\midrule\n878\topen middle drawer & 0.197 & 0.221 & $+$0.024 \\\\\n879\topen top drawer + bowl & 0.265 & 0.340 & $+$0.075 \\\\\n880\tpush plate to stove & 0.190 & 0.197 & $+$0.007 \\\\\n881\tput bowl on plate & 0.375 & 0.474 & $+$0.099 \\\\\n882\tput bowl on stove & 0.235 & 0.395 & $+$0.160 \\\\\n883\tput bowl on cabinet & 0.256 & 0.457 & $+$0.201 \\\\\n884\tput cream cheese in bowl & 0.186 & 0.345 & $+$0.159 \\\\\n885\tput wine on rack & 0.189 & 0.424 & $+$0.235 \\\\\n886\tput wine on cabinet & 0.279 & 0.353 & $+$0.074 \\\\\n887\tturn on stove & 0.385 & 0.421 & $+$0.036 \\\\\n888\t\\midrule\n889\tMean $\\pm$ std & $0.256 \\pm 0.070$ & $0.363 \\pm 0.088$ & $+$0.107 \\\\\n890\t\\bottomrule\n891\t\\end{tabular}\n892\t\\end{table}\n893\t\n894\t\\subsection{Task Complexity and Attention Dependence}\n895\t\\label{sec:task_complexity}\n896\t\n897\tWe classify tasks as \\emph{instruction-discriminative} when multiple tasks share the same manipulated object(s), requiring instruction content to disambiguate the target action (e.g., ``put bowl on plate'' vs.\\ ``put bowl on stove''); remaining tasks involve unique object-action pairings recoverable from visual context alone.\n898\tTable~\\ref{tab:task_complexity} reports per-task $|\\Delta R^2|$ under both groupings.\n899\t\n900\t\\begin{table}[h]\n901\t\\centering\n902\t\\caption{Task-level L1 ablation effects ($|\\Delta R^2|$) grouped by instruction dependence. \\emph{Primary} (conservative): cream cheese in bowl classified as visual-sufficient ($2.5\\times$, $p = 0.033$). \\emph{Sensitivity} (shared-object criterion): cream cheese reclassified as instruction-discriminative ($6.5\\times$, $p = 0.008$).}\n903\t\\label{tab:task_complexity}\n904\t\\small\n905\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n906\t\\toprule\n907\tTask & $|\\Delta R^2|$ & Group \\\\\n908\t\\midrule\n909\tput wine on rack & 0.235 & Instr-disc \\\\\n910\tput bowl on cabinet & 0.201 & Instr-disc \\\\\n911\tput bowl on stove & 0.160 & Instr-disc \\\\\n912\tput bowl on plate & 0.099 & Instr-disc \\\\\n913\topen top drawer + bowl & 0.075 & Instr-disc \\\\\n914\tput wine on cabinet & 0.074 & Instr-disc \\\\\n915\t\\midrule\n916\tput cream cheese in bowl$^\\dagger$ & 0.159 & Vis-suff \\\\\n917\tturn on stove & 0.036 & Vis-suff \\\\\n918\topen middle drawer & 0.024 & Vis-suff \\\\\n919\tpush plate to stove & 0.007 & Vis-suff \\\\\n920\t\\midrule\n921\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Primary}: Instr-disc mean $0.141$; Vis-suff mean $0.057$} \\\\\n922\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Mann-Whitney $U = 21.0$, $p = 0.033$, ratio $= 2.49\\times$} \\\\\n923\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Sensitivity}$^\\dagger$: cream cheese as Instr-disc: $p = 0.008$, $6.5\\times$} \\\\\n924\t\\bottomrule\n925\t\\end{tabular}\n926\t\\end{table}\n927\t\n928\t\\paragraph{Leave-one-out sensitivity.}\n929\tAll leave-one-out $p$-values remain $\\leq 0.083$ (marginally significant), with no single task removal reversing the effect direction.\n930\tReclassifying the boundary case (cream cheese in bowl) to the instruction-discriminative group strengthens the result ($p = 0.008$).\n931\t\n932\tBootstrap 95\\% CI on the complex/simple ratio: $[1.03, 11.12]$ (10{,}000 resamples). The wide interval reflects low statistical power from $N=10$ tasks split into groups of 6 and 4; the point estimate ($2.5\\times$) persists under leave-one-out analysis (all $p \\leq 0.083$, marginally significant; \\S\\ref{sec:task_complexity}).\n933\t\n934\t\\subsection{Specificity Control: Matched-Size Head Ablation}\n935\tTo test whether ablation effects are specific to instruction-dominant heads, we ablate four matched-size subsets of 24 heads at L1: instruction-dominant (highest instruction attention fraction), image-dominant (highest image attention fraction), random (5 seeds), and bottom-24 (lowest instruction attention fraction; overlaps 16/24 heads with the instruction-dominant group).\n936\t\n937\t\\begin{table}[h]\n938\t\\centering\n939\t\\caption{Matched-size ablation controls: trajectory disruption ($d$) from ablating 24-head subsets at L1, ranked by instruction attention fraction. Instruction-dominant heads produce the largest effect, but the modest gap ($8\\%$ over random) indicates distributed, attention-graded redistribution rather than a dedicated subset.}\n940\t\\label{tab:specificity_control}\n941\t\\small\n942\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n943\t\\toprule\n944\tCondition (24 heads) & Cohen's $d$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n945\t\\midrule\n946\tInstruction-dominant & 0.650 & (reference) \\\\\n947\tRandom (5 seeds) & $0.601 \\pm 0.021$ & $[0.568, 0.622]$ \\\\\n948\tImage-dominant & 0.580 & $[0.547, 0.613]$ \\\\\n949\tBottom-24 & 0.565 & --- \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EShKLG3tA1TJXTqiXRAZYQ","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"57847238-255a-4fed-b361-f588fca0fa4a","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:34:42.308Z"}
