{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012ZFWdi9VvfCkkfUGN11sEF","type":"tool_result","content":"600\t\\toprule\n601\tComparison & $\\Delta$ & 95\\% CI & $p$ \\\\\n602\t\\midrule\n603\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\emph{RSA ($\\rho$)}} \\\\\n604\tBest-vs-best image\\_mean & $+$0.017 & incl.\\ 0 & n.s. \\\\\n605\t\\quad (trained L12 vs.\\ reference L0) & & & \\\\\n606\tSame-layer L12 image\\_mean & $+$0.079 & excl.\\ 0 & $<$0.0001 \\\\\n607\t\\quad (trained vs.\\ reference) & & & \\\\\n608\tInstruction effect (max $|\\Delta|$) & 0.006 & --- & n.s. \\\\\n609\t\\midrule\n610\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\emph{Probe $R^2$}} \\\\\n611\tBest-vs-best image\\_mean & $+$0.015 & excl.\\ 0 & 0.006 \\\\\n612\t\\quad (trained L8 vs.\\ reference L0) & & & \\\\\n613\tBest-vs-best readout & $+$0.069 & excl.\\ 0 & $<$0.0001 \\\\\n614\t\\quad (trained L13 vs.\\ reference L32) & & & \\\\\n615\t\\bottomrule\n616\t\\end{tabular}\n617\t\\end{table}\n618\t\n619\t\\subsection{Training Effect Size Summary}\n620\tTable~\\ref{tab:training_effect_summary} consolidates training effects across all metrics.\n621\tTraining effects on image\\_mean metrics are modest (RSA $\\Delta = +0.017$, $R^2$ $\\Delta = +0.015$), but effects on readout metrics are substantial (instruction attention $+18$pp, attention $+46$pp, $R^2$ $\\Delta = +0.069$).\n622\tThis asymmetry indicates that training primarily changes how the readout token integrates information rather than altering the visual representations carried by image tokens.\n623\t\n624\t\\begin{table}[h]\n625\t\\centering\n626\t\\caption{Training effect sizes across metrics. Image\\_mean effects are modest; readout effects are large. Training targets readout-token information integration.}\n627\t\\label{tab:training_effect_summary}\n628\t\\small\n629\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n630\t\\toprule\n631\tMetric & Trained & Reference & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n632\t\\midrule\n633\tRSA peak (image\\_mean) & 0.628 (L12) & 0.612 (L0) & $+$0.017 \\\\\n634\t$R^2$ peak (image\\_mean) & 0.655 (L8) & 0.640 (L0) & $+$0.015 \\\\\n635\t$R^2$ peak (readout) & 0.678 (L13) & 0.609 (L32) & $+$0.069 \\\\\n636\tInstruction attention L31 & 100\\% & 82\\% & $+$18pp \\\\\n637\tInstruction attention L1 & 58\\% & 12\\% & $+$46pp \\\\\n638\tProgress classification & 86.3\\% (L20) & 85.3\\% (L4) & $+$1pp \\\\\n639\t\\bottomrule\n640\t\\end{tabular}\n641\t\\end{table}\n642\t\n643\t\\subsection{Instruction Classification Probes}\n644\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Classification: Dense Layer Profile}\n645\t\\label{sec:dense_routing}\n646\t\n647\tTable~\\ref{tab:dense_routing} shows the dense per-layer profile of cross-instruction classification (10-way) at the readout token for layers L0--L8.\n648\tThe step-function onset between L0 ($10\\%$, chance) and L1 (${\\geq}99.6\\%$) reflects the high discriminability of instruction embeddings from the frozen tokenizer: even incidental attention (${\\sim}12\\%$) suffices for linear classification at L1.\n649\tThe VLA-trained model sustains $100\\%$ through L31; Llama-2 base reaches $100\\%$ by L5 and maintains it. The OXE-pretrained reference and random initialization both decay to ${\\sim}82\\%$ by L31, indicating that deep-layer instruction maintenance requires either VLA training or structured language-model pretraining on text-only data.\n650\t\n651\t\\begin{table}[h]\n652\t\\centering\n653\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy at readout token, L0--L8. Values reflect dense classification evaluation; the main text (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}) reports sparse single-split results, yielding minor differences (e.g., L8: $98.2\\%$ vs.\\ $98.6\\%$).}\n654\t\\label{tab:dense_routing}\n655\t\\small\n656\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n657\t\\toprule\n658\tLayer & Trained & Reference & Image\\_mean (trained) \\\\\n659\t\\midrule\n660\tL0 & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n661\tL1 & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n662\tL2 & 100.0\\% & 99.6\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n663\tL3 & 100.0\\% & 99.6\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n664\tL4 & 100.0\\% & 99.1\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n665\tL5 & 100.0\\% & 98.2\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n666\tL6 & 100.0\\% & 97.3\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n667\tL7 & 100.0\\% & 96.4\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n668\tL8 & 100.0\\% & 98.6\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n669\t\\bottomrule\n670\t\\end{tabular}\n671\t\\end{table}\n672\t\n673\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction LOOCV Validation}\n674\t\\label{sec:loocv}\n675\t\n676\tLeave-one-out cross-validation over 10 folds (each fold holds out one task's instructions) validates that the cross-instruction probe result is not driven by memorization.\n677\tTrained readout achieves $100\\% \\pm 0.00$ across all 10 folds at layers L1--L31 (perfect generalization).\n678\t\n679\t\\subsubsection{QA Probe: Progress and Subtask Classification}\n680\tTo test whether LLM layers encode task-relevant semantic information beyond instruction identity, we train logistic regression probes (scikit-learn, $C = 1.0$, max\\_iter $= 1{,}000$) to classify task progress (4-class: each trajectory split into 4 equal temporal segments) and active subtask (binary: first vs.\\ second half) from frozen representations, with an 80/20 random train/test split (Table~\\ref{tab:qa_probe}).\n681\t\n682\t\\begin{table}[h]\n683\t\\centering\n684\t\\caption{QA probe accuracy (progress + subtask classification, best layer).}\n685\t\\label{tab:qa_probe}\n686\t\\small\n687\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n688\t\\toprule\n689\tSource & Best Layer & Accuracy \\\\\n690\t\\midrule\n691\tTrained readout & L20 & 86.3\\% \\\\\n692\tReference readout & L20 & 85.3\\% \\\\\n693\tDINOv2 cls\\_token & --- & 82.7\\% \\\\\n694\t\\bottomrule\n695\t\\end{tabular}\n696\t\\end{table}\n697\t\n698\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Probe Profiles}\n699\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n700\t\\centering\n701\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_probe_conditions.pdf}\n702\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy at the readout token across all 32 LLM layers. VLA-trained achieves $99.6\\%$ at L1, saturating to $100\\%$. Llama-2 base starts at $80.2\\%$, reaching $100\\%$ by L5. Reference starts at $100\\%$ but decays to $82\\%$ by L31.}\n703\t\\end{figure}\n704\t\n705\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Architecture Probe and Attention Profile}\n706\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_probe} reports cross-instruction probe accuracy and L1 instruction-dominant head counts across six conditions spanning two architectures.\n707\tThe probe-attention dissociation at L1 is notable: TracVLA training slightly \\emph{reduces} instruction-dominant heads ($32 \\to 27$--$30/32$) while \\emph{decreasing} probe accuracy ($98.1\\% \\to 83.6\\%$), consistent with representational reorganization rather than linear separability.\n708\t\n709\t\\begin{table}[h]\n710\t\\centering\n711\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way probe accuracy and L1 instruction-dominant heads across six conditions.\n712\tProbe accuracy and attention head count are dissociated at L1: TracVLA training slightly reduces heads ($32 \\to 27$--$30/32$) while decreasing probe accuracy ($98.1\\% \\to 83.6\\%$), paralleling the information-computation dissociation observed in OpenVLA.\n713\tImage\\_mean remains at chance ($10\\%$) in all conditions (omitted).}\n714\t\\label{tab:cross_arch_probe}\n715\t\\small\n716\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n717\t\\toprule\n718\tCondition & L1 heads & L1 & L2+ & L31 & Mode \\\\\n719\t\\midrule\n720\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{OpenVLA (Llama-2 7B)}} \\\\\n721\t\\quad Random init & 0/32 & 100.0\\% & decays & 82.0\\% & incid.+forget \\\\\n722\t\\quad Llama-2 base & 9/32 & 80.2\\% & L5: 100\\% & 100.0\\% & gradual+stable \\\\\n723\t\\quad VLA trained & 24/32 & 99.6\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & immed.+stable \\\\\n724\t\\quad LIBERO-finetuned & 22/32 & 99.7\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & immed.+stable \\\\\n725\t\\midrule\n726\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{TraceVLA (Phi-3-V 4B)}} \\\\\n727\t\\quad Phi-3-Vision & 32/32 & 98.1\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & inherited+stable \\\\\n728\t\\quad VLA trained & 27--30/32 & 83.6\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & reorg.+stable \\\\\n729\t\\bottomrule\n730\t\\end{tabular}\n731\t\\end{table}\n732\t\n733\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n734\t\\centering\n735\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cross_architecture_attention.pdf}\n736\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads across layers for two architectures (OpenVLA / TraceVLA), each comparing pre-VLA and VLA-trained conditions. Despite opposite starting points---OpenVLA (Llama-2) begins with sparse attention while TraceVLA (Phi-3-V) begins with distributed attention---VLA training drives both to entry-exit concentration (L1 saturation, mid-layer suppression, L29--L31 re-engagement).}\n737\t\\end{figure}\n738\t\n739\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Architecture L1 Instruction Dominance}\n740\tFigure~\\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1_bar} complements the main-text summary (Figure~\\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1}) by showing the fraction of instruction-dominant heads at L1 across all three architectures in a single comparison. The trained/reference fractions are comparable within each architecture (OpenVLA: 23/32 vs.\\ 26/32; TracVLA: 27/32 vs.\\ 32/32; NORA: 13/16 vs.\\ 13/16), consistent with L1 instruction attention being largely inherited from the pretrained backbone rather than acquired during VLA fine-tuning.\n741\t\n742\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n743\t\\centering\n744\t\\includegraphics[width=0.65\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cross_arch_l1.pdf}\n745\t\\caption{\\textbf{L1 instruction-dominant head fractions across three architectures.} Trained and reference conditions show comparable fractions in all three VLAs, indicating that L1 instruction attention largely pre-exists in the pretrained backbone.}\n746\t\\label{fig:cross_arch_l1_bar}\n747\t\\end{figure}\n748\t\n749\t\\subsubsection{L1 Attention Pattern Analysis}\n750\tFigure~\\ref{fig:attention_appendix} shows the per-head L1 attention distribution at the readout token.\n751\tThe three-level gradient (randomly initialized 0/32, Llama-2 base 9/32, VLA-trained 24/32 instruction-dominant heads) shows that language pretraining seeds partial instruction attention and VLA fine-tuning shifts it further toward instruction dominance.\n752\t\n753\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n754\t\\centering\n755\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_stacked_bar.pdf}\n756\t\\caption{L1 attention from the readout token across three conditions, per head (sorted by instruction attention fraction). VLA-trained: 24/32 instruction-dominant heads ($57.7\\%$ text attention). Llama-2 base: 9/32 heads ($36.5\\%$). Randomly initialized: 0/32 heads ($11.7\\%$). See Table~\\ref{tab:multilayer_attention} for quantitative comparison across layers.}\n757\t\\label{fig:attention_appendix}\n758\t\\end{figure}\n759\t\n760\t\\subsubsection{Multi-Layer Attention Patterns}\n761\tTable~\\ref{tab:multilayer_attention} shows a 4-layer subset of the full 32-layer attention profile.\n762\tThe full profile (Figure~\\ref{fig:multilayer_attention}) reveals three qualitatively distinct patterns: the randomly initialized model maintains 0 instruction-dominant heads at all 32 layers; Llama-2 base shows a mid-depth peak (L9--L16: 15--22 heads), consistent with typical autoregressive LM processing depth; VLA fine-tuning produces an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1: 21--24 heads, L31: 18 heads, intermediate layers 0--11).\n763\t\n764\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n765\t\\centering\n766\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_multi_layer_attn.pdf}\n767\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and mean text attention fraction across layers for three conditions. VLA-trained shows elevated values at L1 and L31; Llama-2 base peaks at L16; randomly initialized remains near zero throughout.}\n768\t\\label{fig:multilayer_attention}\n769\t\\end{figure}\n770\t\n771\t\\begin{table}[h]\n772\t\\centering\n773\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and text attention fraction at four sampled layers (subset of the 32-layer profile in Figure~\\ref{fig:multilayer_attention}). VLA-trained shows entry-exit concentration (L1, L31); Llama-2 base peaks at mid-depth (L16); randomly initialized shows no instruction attention. Head counts from the 32-layer bulk extraction may differ slightly from dedicated per-layer analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}) due to threshold boundary effects.}\n774\t\\label{tab:multilayer_attention}\n775\t\\small\n776\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n777\t\\toprule\n778\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{VLA Trained} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Llama-2 base} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Rand.\\ Init} \\\\\n779\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5} \\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\n780\tLayer & Heads & Text\\% & Heads & Text\\% & Heads & Text\\% \\\\\n781\t\\midrule\n782\tL1 & \\textbf{24/32} & 57.7 & 9/32 & 36.5 & 0/32 & 11.7 \\\\\n783\tL8 & 4/32 & 46.8 & 8/32 & 48.8 & 0/32 & 7.9 \\\\\n784\tL16 & 10/32 & 48.4 & \\textbf{25/32} & 60.2 & 0/32 & 9.2 \\\\\n785\tL31 & \\textbf{18/32} & 56.3 & 8/32 & 34.9 & 0/32 & 7.6 \\\\\n786\t\\bottomrule\n787\t\\end{tabular}\n788\t\\end{table}\n789\t\n790\t\\subsubsection{Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}\n791\t\\label{sec:threshold_sensitivity}\n792\t\n793\tThe main text defines instruction-dominant heads as those allocating $>50\\%$ attention to instruction tokens.\n794\tTable~\\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity} shows that the three-condition gradient (randomly initialized $<$ Llama-2 base $<$ VLA-trained) holds across all thresholds from 30\\% to 70\\%; the 50\\% threshold is not a boundary-sensitive choice.\n795\t\n796\t\\begin{table}[h]\n797\t\\centering\n798\t\\caption{Number of instruction-dominant heads at L1 under varying attention thresholds. The three-condition ordering is stable across all thresholds.}\n799\t\\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgJQjdAbFKM8Sgf8v3eXT3","session_id":"a551ef02-7e02-4516-a695-4e220ef1b867","uuid":"e80f9768-9de0-4d12-b34d-954eb7986daa","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:35:02.598Z"}
